                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the scaling gap`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `The contract is transformational: <strong>AR$4,200M</strong> in incremental revenue (the firm does AR$9,600M ≈ US$8.0M today). But Don Aldo personally approves every PO over AR$2M, holds the <strong>only</strong> banking signature, and keeps every supplier and buyer relationship in his head. Separate the two gaps: the <strong>capital gap</strong> (AR$1,500M CapEx + AR$860M trapped on 75-day terms) and the <strong>capability gap</strong> (no second-line authority). With only AR$700M of free cash flow a year, estimate the runway in months — <em>because runway, not margin, governs survival in a scale-up</em>. Which constraint binds first?`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Professionalize management`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Aldo's surgery is in June; if he is out for two weeks, purchasing, banking and key accounts stall. Decide who leads, then — crucially — how much real decision authority actually moves. An external GM costs <strong>AR$90M/year</strong>, but a GM who can't sign a cheque while Aldo keeps the only signature and the AR$2M PO threshold is <em>an expensive spectator</em>. Reset the banking signatories and PO limits so the firm runs without him, and build the dedicated key-account / EDI team the retailer demands. <strong>Professionalization is transferring decision rights, not adding headcount.</strong>`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Finance the growth without losing control`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `You need ~<strong>AR$2,360M</strong> (AR$1,500M CapEx + AR$860M working capital). Mix bank debt (leasing the tunnel — keeps control but pushes leverage from 0.8x toward 3.1x EBITDA), supplier/retailer finance (eases the 75-day trap), retained earnings (slow, finite), and a regional PE fund's <strong>AR$1,200M for 30%</strong>. Remember: <em>the 75-day terms mean the cash arrives after the loan instalments are due</em> — control of what, if the bank controls your covenants? Recommend go / scale-down / decline, and if you take the PE stake, negotiate the board seat, CapEx veto, drag-along and exit clause — <strong>the terms shape control more than the headline 30%</strong>.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Open the succession question`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Aldo's surgery is weeks away and there is no shareholders' agreement, no protocol, no defined successor — and Lucía (34) and Tomás (31) each assume they are next. <strong>Succession is a continuity risk to be designed before a shock, not after.</strong> Put in place the governance architecture — family protocol, shareholders' agreement, a family council, board independence — and a realistic, merit-based development path with an interim cover plan for the surgery. The family council is in the room. <em>Treat family relationships as a strategic asset to protect, or watch a forced transition fracture both the firm and Sunday lunch.</em>`,

                    r1_gap_label:           `What is the FIRST binding constraint on scaling Don Aldo?`,
                    r1_runway_label:        `Estimate the current cash runway under the contract's working-capital draw (months).`,
                    r1_separate_label:      `How clearly do you separate the capability gap from the capital gap in the diagnosis? (%)`,
                    r1_separate_readout:    `Clarity in separating capability vs capital:`,
                    gap_capability:         `A capability gap — one founder approving every PO and holding the only signature cannot run AR$4,200M of new demand`,
                    gap_capital:            `A capital gap — find the AR$1,500M CapEx and AR$860M working capital and the rest follows`,
                    gap_demand:             `A demand gap — the issue is winning the listing; capacity and structure are details`,
                    gap_margin:             `A margin gap — fix the 11% EBITDA first, the frozen line will fund itself`,
                    runway_tight:           `Tight — roughly 9–12 months once AR$1,500M CapEx and AR$860M of trapped working capital land before the 75-day cash arrives`,
                    runway_loose:           `Around 18–24 months — the CapEx is large but the contract revenue covers it quickly`,
                    runway_never:           `Runway is fine — AR$700M of free cash flow each year is plenty`,

                    r2_leader_label:        `Who leads day-to-day once Don Aldo steps back?`,
                    r2_delegate_label:      `How much real decision authority do you actually transfer? (%)`,
