                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_capability:    `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> Capability — not capital — is the first binding constraint. One founder approving every PO and holding the only signature cannot run AR$4,200M of new demand, especially with a surgery in June. Capital follows once you have a structure that can absorb it.`,
                    coach_r1_capital:       `<strong>Capital matters, but it's the second constraint, not the first.</strong> Find every peso of CapEx and working capital and you still have one signatory on a hospital bed. Money can't approve a Monday cheque. Name the capability gap first.`,
                    coach_r1_demand:        `<strong>Demand isn't your problem — the retailer is already offering AR$4,200M.</strong> The problem is whether a founder-dependent structure can deliver it. Winning the listing you can't operate just moves the crisis three weeks later.`,
                    coach_r1_margin:        `<strong>Margin is not the binding constraint here.</strong> An 11% EBITDA is healthy for LATAM food processing; the scale-up dies on cash and capability, not on points of margin. Re-read the founder-dependency map.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the capability gap as the first constraint AND priced the runway honestly — 9–12 months once the CapEx and the AR$860M trapped on 75-day terms land before the cash returns. That is exactly the lens that separates a profitable contract from a survivable one.`,
                    coach_r1_runwaynaive:   `<strong>'AR$700M of free cash flow is plenty' is the trap.</strong> The contract consumes AR$1,500M of CapEx and traps AR$860M before the 75-day cash arrives — multiples of your annual FCF. Profitable growth on extended terms can starve the firm; count the runway in months.`,
                    coach_r1_blurred:       `<strong>Blurring the two gaps hides the real risk.</strong> If you treat capability and capital as one 'growth problem', you'll throw money at a structure that can't absorb it. Separate them: one is solved with a financing mix, the other with delegated authority.`,
                    coach_r2_gmauthority:   `<strong>That's professionalization, not just hiring.</strong> An external GM with real decision rights and a management committee builds the spine the firm has been missing. Now the AR$90M buys you authority that runs the firm when Aldo can't.`,
                    coach_r2_gmpartial:     `<strong>You've started moving authority — keep going.</strong> A GM with partial decision rights is better than a figurehead, but until the banking signature and PO limits move too, Aldo is still the single point of failure.`,
                    coach_r2_spectator:     `<strong>You hired a GM at AR$90M and delegated almost nothing.</strong> Aldo still approves every PO over AR$2M and holds the only signature — your GM is an expensive spectator. What did you actually delegate? Headcount isn't depth.`,
                    coach_r2_heir:          `<strong>Promoting an untested heir into the top job under crisis is a family landmine.</strong> Lucía and Tomás have never had formal authority; anointing one now, with no protocol and a surgery looming, risks fracturing the family before it builds the spine. Develop them — don't crown one in a panic.`,
                    coach_r2_aldo:          `<strong>Leaving Aldo in sole charge is the founder's trap, fully sprung.</strong> Three weeks after signing he's in surgery and no one else can sign a cheque or talk to the buyer. You have AR$4,200M of demand and one decision-maker on a hospital bed. Who signs on Monday?`,
                    coach_r2_signatory:     `<strong>The banking signature is the single point of failure.</strong> While Aldo holds the only signature and personally clears every PO over AR$2M, the EDI orders stall the moment he's unavailable. Add a second signatory and tiered limits — that's the delegation that actually matters.`,
                    coach_r2_nokeyaccount:  `<strong>A national EDI listing can't run off the old sales desk.</strong> The retailer requires EDI ordering and a dedicated key-account team; without an owner for that relationship, the contract you fought for breaks on its first reorder cycle.`,
                    coach_r2_trap:          `<strong>This is the founder's trap in numbers.</strong> You added a senior layer and its cost, but left the signature and the AR$2M PO threshold with Aldo and delegated almost nothing. You're paying for professionalization without getting it — the most expensive way to change nothing.`,
                    coach_r3_alldebt:       `<strong>You kept 100% ownership and took on heavy debt.</strong> Leverage just went from 0.8x toward ~3.1x EBITDA, and the 75-day retailer terms mean the cash arrives after the loan instalments are due. Control of what, exactly, if the bank controls your covenants? Debt has a cash dimension, not just an equity one.`,
                    coach_r3_negotiated:    `<strong>You took the capital AND protected control.</strong> Negotiating the board seat, the CapEx veto, the drag-along and the exit clause means the PE stake solves cash without handing over the control you said mattered most. The terms, not the headline 30%, decide who really runs the firm.`,
                    coach_r3_headline:      `<strong>You took AR$1,200M for 30% — but did you read the term sheet?</strong> A drag-along, a board seat with a veto over CapEx, and a 5-year exit clause. You solved cash and gave away the very control you said mattered. The protections you skipped are the ones that bite later.`,
                    coach_r3_scaledown:     `<strong>Prudent — but prudence has a price.</strong> You took a third of the volume and kept it simple. Meanwhile the Córdoba rival takes the national listing, doubles its plant, and out-buys you on every input. Under-investing in growth is itself a strategic risk; you may have handed a competitor the scale to undercut you at home.`,
                    coach_r3_decline:       `<strong>You protected the balance sheet — and handed the listing to Córdoba.</strong> The rival doubles its plant and now out-buys you on every input in your own region. Avoiding the risk of scaling is not the same as avoiding risk; sometimes the safe choice is the dangerous one.`,
                    coach_r3_balanced:      `<strong>A blended mix is the defensible play.</strong> Lean on supplier/retailer finance to ease the 75-day trap, keep debt below the covenant cliff, and use equity only if you negotiate the terms. The right answer balances speed against control — and keeps the runway above zero.`,
                    coach_r4_proactive:     `<strong>That's continuity discipline.</strong> Designing the governance and succession step before the surgery — not after — is what turns a founder health shock from a crisis into a planned handover. Family relationships are a strategic asset; you just protected one.`,
                    coach_r4_signal:        `<strong>A verbal signal with nothing documented is barely better than silence.</strong> If Aldo is incapacitated next month, an undocumented intention won't direct the bank, the board or the family. Put it in a protocol and a shareholders' agreement before June.`,
                    coach_r4_defer:         `<strong>You parked succession for 'after the contract stabilizes' — but the surgery is in June.</strong> There's no protocol, and Lucía and Tomás each assume they're next. If Aldo is out for a month, who runs the firm, and what does that do to the family at Sunday lunch? Succession is designed before the shock, not after.`,
                    coach_r4_nogovernance:  `<strong>A 'family understanding' is not governance.</strong> Without a protocol, a shareholders' agreement and a council, the first disagreement under stress becomes a legal and emotional fight. Architecture is what lets a firm scale and transition without fracturing.`,
                    coach_r4_pick:          `<strong>Anointing one and telling the other to fall in line is how families fracture.</strong> Lucía and Tomás both work in the business with no formal authority; a unilateral pick, with no merit path or protocol, risks losing one of them — and the peace at the table.`,
                    coach_r4_avoid:         `<strong>Leaving both undefined doesn't keep the peace — it defers the fight.</strong> Ambiguity under a health shock is its own crisis: two presumed successors, no plan, and a bank that needs one signature. Define roles and a development path, not a vacuum.`,
                    coach_r4_continuity:    `<strong>This is the textbook transition.</strong> Proactive timing, full governance architecture, and a merit-based development path with interim cover for the surgery — you treated succession as a business-continuity discipline and family harmony as a strategic asset, exactly as Fraile frames it.`,
                    coach_r4_lowconviction: `<strong>Half-hearted succession work is succession deferred.</strong> If you treat governance as a box to tick rather than a continuity discipline, the documents won't hold when the shock comes. Commit to it as a business decision, because that is what it is.`,

